---
title: "Suggesting planet for plnet with qgram"
author: Babbaragga
date: 2026-11-17
abstract: Use overlapping character pairs to suggest a catalogue entry after a missing keystroke.
---

A reader types `plnet` into a catalogue containing `plane`, `brick`, `planet` and `plum`. All but `brick` share the prefix `pl`. The ending gives us another clue. The `qgram` module lets us use both clues in a short suggestion function.

## Count the shared pairs

Call `qgrams("plnet", 2)` to slide a two-character window across the word. The module pads each end with the character `chr(1)`, so the first and last letters get their own pairs. Writing that character as `·` on paper, the two words give us

```text
plnet    ·p  pl  ln  ne  et  t·
planet   ·p  pl  la  an  ne  et  t·
```

Deleting the `a` replaces `la` and `an` with `ln`. Five distinct pairs survive. The two words contain eight distinct pairs between them, so their Jaccard similarity is exactly 5/8, or 0.625. On the same query, `plane` scores 3/9 and `plum` scores 2/9. A cutoff of 0.5 keeps just `planet`.

## Filter a small catalogue

```lateralus
fn suggestions(typed: str, words: list, cutoff: float) -> list {
    let hits = []
    for word in words {
        if jaccard(typed, word, 2) >= cutoff {
            hits = hits + [word]
        }
    }
    return hits
}
```

The function returns matches in catalogue order. Raising the cutoff to 0.75 would hide the intended match here. Choose a cutoff against the misspellings your own catalogue needs to catch.

## Run the example and its pinned vector

From the repository root, use Python 3.10 or newer. The commands combine the shipped module and the example in a temporary directory so the compiler sees their functions in one source file.

```sh
demo_dir=$(mktemp -d)
cat stdlib/qgram.ltl examples/qgram_typo_search.ltl > "$demo_dir/search.ltl"
PYTHONPATH="$PWD" python3 -m lateralus_lang run "$demo_dir/search.ltl"
```

The example checks the exact padded pairs, the 0.625 score, the single match at 0.5 and the empty result at 0.75 before printing one line.

```text
planet
```

Try a transposition by changing the query in the final call to `plnaet`. Predict which adjacent pairs it loses, then run it again. Leave the pinned vector unchanged so the original case keeps checking the module. [Runnable example](../../examples/qgram_typo_search.ltl) · [qgram source](../../stdlib/qgram.ltl)
